9
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:14,740
So was every invention in history.

10
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
A.G.I. would be the last.

11
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:19,810
Artificial general intelligence:

12
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:20,990
a machine that can do

13
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:22,600
what a human mind does.

14
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:24,090
Including invent.

15
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:26,530
From then on, it invents for us.

16
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:28,320
Faster than we ever could.

17
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:30,520
So what will it invent for you?

18
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:32,900
The inventing has already begun.

19
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,530
This year, an A.I. found

20
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:36,890
an answer no human had found.

21
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,830
To a puzzle about dots on a page.

22
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:40,800
Guess how long

23
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,000
it had been waiting.

24
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:44,010
Eighty years.

25
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,730
A mathematician who reviewed

26
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:48,150
the answer called it a milestone.

27
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:50,340
But the idea of a final invention

28
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:52,590
goes back to a single sentence.

29
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:53,960
Half promise.

30
00:00:54,070 --> 00:00:55,180
Half condition.

31
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,550
And the condition is up to us.

32
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:00,070
Here is the promise.

33
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:00,990
"The first

34
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:02,560
ultra-intelligent machine

35
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:03,980
is the last invention

36
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:05,800
that man need ever make."

37
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,190
It's more than 60 years old.

38
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:09,510
Jack Good wrote it.

39
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:10,920
In the Second World War,

40
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:11,720
he helped break

41
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:12,920
German naval codes

42
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:14,110
