43
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,270
His first boss there

44
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:16,610
was Alan Turing.

45
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:18,850
The logic: a machine smarter

46
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:20,180
than us can design

47
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:21,330
a better machine.

48
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,570
And that one, a better one still.

49
00:01:23,890 --> 00:01:25,250
It sounds like an ending.

50
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,540
But our survival, Good wrote,

51
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:29,670
depends on building this machine.

52
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:31,770
He even guessed when:

53
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:34,030
"within the twentieth century."

54
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:36,230
About the date, he was wrong.

55
00:01:36,410 --> 00:01:37,730
So why should you take

56
00:01:37,770 --> 00:01:39,130
him seriously today?

57
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:40,740
Because today, people are

58
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:42,380
trying to build that machine.

59
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:44,600
They call it A.G.I.

60
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,410
Demis Hassabis co-founded

61
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:47,840
Google DeepMind,

62
00:01:47,910 --> 00:01:49,790
so he has a stake in this.

63
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:51,010
His plan:

64
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:53,540
It was literally step one, solve

65
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:55,990
intelligence, step two, use it to

66
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:58,440
solve everything else. And people

67
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,970
were quite confused. But we really

68
